You know that when you enter a room you can see everything in it (if it is
light, obvoiusly), and that when you leave it it still is on the map.
This means that the program cares to map the dungeon for you. This is not
very realistic. When you leave the room, the room must disappear from the
screen, unless you have explicitly asked to the program to record it in the
map. This will cost you some time (the time to write it down on a -simulated-
piece of paper). This can be interesting because it make some additions to
the game:

- mapping costs time
- it can be implemented an automapping mode. You move slower with automapping
  enabled.
- the player is forced to make a compromise between movement speed and
  mapping.
- Piece of papers (blank paper scrolls?) are needed to map levels. If the
  player hasn't any piece of paper he cannot map the level. He can erase
  scrolls or previously used maps. A player can start with a pack of papers
  to use for mapping levels (don't forget a pencil!).

HACKers, what do you think about it? Any comment to rec.games.hack.
